---

<IndividualPageLayout
    title={seoTitle}
    description={seoDescription}
    canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
    robots="index,follow,max-image-preview:large,max-snippet:-1,max-video-preview:-1"

        modifiedDate={dates.modified}
publishedDate = {dates.published}

    pageTitle={pageTitle}
    heroImage="/media/precision-cnc-machining-metal-manufacturing-workshop-hero.webp"
    heroImageAlt="Precision manufacturing and CNC machining of titanium alloy components"
    heroImageWidth={1920}
    heroImageHeight={1080}
    socialImage={socialImage}
    socialImageAlt="Titanium alloys for casting, forging, and precision machining"
    faqSectionTitle="Frequently Asked Questions: Titanium Alloys"
    faqItems={faqs}
    ctaTitle="Review a Titanium Manufacturing Program"
    ctaDescription="Send your drawing, titanium grade, governing specification, service environment, quantity, casting or forging route, heat treatment, machining, surface treatment, inspection, and documentation requirements for an engineering review."
    ctaButtonText="Request a Titanium Review"
    sourcesTitle="Technical References"
    sourcesList={sources}
    resourcesTitle="Related Titanium Materials, Manufacturing and Quality Resources"
    resourcesList={internalResources}
>
    <div slot="full-width" class="individual-content">

        <section id="overview">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Titanium Alloys for Lightweight, Corrosion-Resistant Components</h2>
                <p>
                    Titanium alloys combine low density, high specific strength, corrosion resistance, fatigue performance, and useful temperature capability. ForceBeyond supports titanium components across
                    <a href="/titanium-investment-casting" class="highlight-link">investment casting</a>,
                    <a href="/titanium-aerospace-forging" class="highlight-link">forging</a>,
                    <a href="/titanium-machining" class="highlight-link">precision machining</a>,
                    heat treatment, surface finishing, inspection, and assembly.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This page serves as the titanium material-family guide for Ti-6Al-4V Grade 5, Ti-6Al-4V ELI Grade 23, commercially pure titanium, and application-specific grades. Final alloy and process selection should be based on product form, specification, service environment, loading, fatigue, temperature, corrosion, cleanliness, and inspection requirements.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="alloy-families">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Titanium Alloy Families and Representative Grades</h2>
                <p>
                    Mechanical properties vary by chemistry, product form, heat treatment, section size, processing route, and test method. The table below is intended for initial material selection rather than final design allowables.
                </p>

                <div class="content-table-wrapper">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col">Titanium Family</th>
                                <th scope="col">Representative Grades</th>
                                <th scope="col">Typical Selection Factors</th>
                                <th scope="col">Representative Applications</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Alpha-Beta Titanium</th>
                                <td>Ti-6Al-4V Grade 5 and related specification-controlled grades</td>
                                <td>High specific strength, fatigue performance, corrosion resistance, forgeability, heat treatment response, and broad industrial availability.</td>
                                <td>Aerospace brackets, airframe fittings, fasteners, shafts, energy hardware, marine components, and industrial structures.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Extra-Low-Interstitial Alpha-Beta Titanium</th>
                                <td>Ti-6Al-4V ELI Grade 23</td>
                                <td>Tighter interstitial limits, improved fracture toughness, low-temperature ductility, and application-specific medical or aerospace requirements.</td>
                                <td>Medical components where approved, cryogenic hardware, aerospace parts, and high-reliability structures.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Commercially Pure Titanium</th>
                                <td>Grades 1, 2, 3, and 4</td>
                                <td>Corrosion resistance, formability, weldability, ductility, and lower strength than alpha-beta alloys.</td>
                                <td>Chemical equipment, heat exchangers, marine hardware, piping, vessels, architectural components, and medical hardware where specified.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Near-Alpha and Beta Titanium</th>
                                <td>Application-specific aerospace, energy, and high-temperature grades</td>
                                <td>Elevated-temperature strength, creep resistance, deep hardenability, formability, or specialized fatigue performance.</td>
                                <td>Engine components, high-temperature structures, springs, fasteners, landing-gear hardware, and specialized aerospace parts.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <p>
                    Review the broader
                    <a href="/materials-technical-specs" class="highlight-link">materials and technical specifications</a>
                    hub for cross-family alloy selection and manufacturing compatibility.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="grade-comparison">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Grade 5 vs. Grade 23 ELI vs. Commercially Pure Titanium</h2>
                <div class="content-table-wrapper">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col">Selection Factor</th>
                                <th scope="col">Grade 5</th>
                                <th scope="col">Grade 23 ELI</th>
                                <th scope="col">Commercially Pure Titanium</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Strength</th>
                                <td>High strength for structural and aerospace applications.</td>
                                <td>Similar alloy family with tighter interstitial control and application-specific property requirements.</td>
                                <td>Lower strength but higher ductility and formability in many conditions.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Fracture Toughness and Ductility</th>
                                <td>Depends on condition, section, processing, and heat treatment.</td>
                                <td>Often selected where lower interstitial content and improved toughness are required.</td>
                                <td>Generally ductile, with properties varying by CP grade.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Corrosion Resistance</th>
                                <td>Strong general corrosion resistance in many environments.</td>
                                <td>Comparable family-level corrosion behavior, subject to surface and environment.</td>
                                <td>Widely selected for chemical and marine corrosion resistance.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Typical Manufacturing Routes</th>
                                <td>Casting, forging, billet machining, additive manufacturing, and combinations.</td>
                                <td>Precision forging, machining, additive manufacturing, and selected casting routes where specification permits.</td>
                                <td>Plate, sheet, bar, tube, fabrication, machining, and selected casting or forming routes.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="manufacturing-route">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Selecting a Titanium Manufacturing Route</h2>
                <p>
                    The correct route depends on geometry, volume, alloy, product form, mechanical properties, cleanliness, inspection, and tooling economics.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><a href="/titanium-investment-casting" class="highlight-link">Titanium investment casting:</a></strong> complex near-net geometry, internal features, reduced machining, and production parts where the alloy and specification permit.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/industrial-titanium-sand-casting" class="highlight-link">Titanium sand casting:</a></strong> larger or lower-volume components with process-specific surface, section, and inspection considerations.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/titanium-aerospace-forging" class="highlight-link">Titanium forging:</a></strong> components requiring grain flow, fatigue, impact, and structural performance.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/titanium-machining" class="highlight-link">Billet and forged-part machining:</a></strong> prototypes, low volume, simple shapes, and applications requiring wrought material properties.</li>
                    <li><strong>Hybrid routes:</strong> casting or forging followed by heat treatment, HIP, machining, chemical milling, finishing, inspection, and assembly.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="casting">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Titanium Casting and Alpha-Case Control</h2>
                <p>
                    Molten and high-temperature titanium reacts strongly with oxygen, nitrogen, hydrogen, carbon, and many conventional refractories.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Atmosphere control:</strong> vacuum or inert-atmosphere processing helps limit gas pickup and oxidation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mold and crucible compatibility:</strong> ceramic, graphite, or other specialized systems must be selected for the alloy and process.</li>
                    <li><strong>Alpha-case allowance:</strong> surface reaction layers may require chemical milling, pickling, grinding, or machining removal.</li>
                    <li><strong>Solidification control:</strong> gating, feeding, hot tearing, shrinkage, and local section changes require process-specific review.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verification:</strong> alpha-case depth, chemistry, microstructure, NDT, and dimensional inspection may be required by specification.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="forging">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Titanium Forging Considerations</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Temperature window:</strong> forging temperature and strain rate affect flow stress, cracking, and microstructure.</li>
                    <li><strong>Grain flow:</strong> die and preform design can align material flow with high-load regions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Surface protection:</strong> scale, contamination, lubrication, and atmosphere must be managed during heating and forging.</li>
                    <li><strong>Heat treatment:</strong> annealing, solution treatment, aging, or stress relief should follow the exact grade and specification.</li>
                    <li><strong>Inspection:</strong> ultrasonic testing, penetrant inspection, dimensional inspection, and mechanical testing may be required.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="machining">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Machining Titanium Alloys</h2>
                <p>
                    Titanium combines low thermal conductivity, chemical reactivity, springback, and strength at cutting temperatures, which can concentrate heat at the cutting edge.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Rigid setups:</strong> control deflection, vibration, chatter, and feature position.</li>
                    <li><strong>Sharp cutting tools:</strong> maintain positive cutting action and avoid rubbing or excessive dwell.</li>
                    <li><strong>Heat management:</strong> use suitable coolant delivery, tool material, geometry, and cutting parameters.</li>
                    <li><strong>Low radial engagement:</strong> selected toolpaths can help reduce heat and maintain stable cutting on complex parts.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/multi-axis-cnc" class="highlight-link">Multi-axis machining:</a></strong> improve access, reduce setups, and use shorter tools on complex titanium components.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="heat-treatment">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Heat Treatment and Microstructure Control</h2>
                <p>
                    Heat treatment affects strength, ductility, fatigue, residual stress, microstructure, and dimensional stability.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Annealing:</strong> reduce residual stress and establish a stable microstructure for subsequent machining or service.</li>
                    <li><strong>Solution treatment and aging:</strong> used on selected titanium alloys to develop specified strength and microstructure.</li>
                    <li><strong>Atmosphere control:</strong> vacuum or inert processing may be required to control oxidation and alpha-case formation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Distortion planning:</strong> fixturing, machining allowance, support, and final inspection should account for thermal movement.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verification:</strong> hardness, tensile testing, microstructure, alpha case, dimensional inspection, or customer-specific checks may be required.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="surface-finishing">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Titanium Surface Finishing and Cleaning</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><a href="/surface-finishing" class="highlight-link">Pickling and chemical milling:</a></strong> remove scale, contamination, and alpha case under controlled conditions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Passivation and cleaning:</strong> support a clean oxide surface and remove processing contamination where required.</li>
                    <li><strong>Anodizing and coating:</strong> selected for color coding, wear, galling, electrical, or corrosion-related requirements.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mechanical finishing:</strong> grinding, polishing, blasting, and deburring for surface, edge, and dimensional requirements.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cleanliness:</strong> final cleaning, handling, packaging, and contamination control may be critical for medical, aerospace, or vacuum applications.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="inspection">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Material Verification, NDT and Documentation</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Material documentation:</strong> mill or material test reports, heat and lot traceability, and certificates of conformity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Positive material identification:</strong> XRF, OES, or project-specific chemistry verification where appropriate.</li>
                    <li><strong>Surface NDT:</strong> penetrant inspection for project-specific surface-discontinuity requirements.</li>
                    <li><strong>Volumetric NDT:</strong> ultrasonic or radiographic inspection based on product form, geometry, defect risk, and specification.</li>
                    <li><strong>Metallurgical testing:</strong> microstructure, alpha case, interstitial content, grain size, hardness, tensile, fatigue, or other project-specific evaluation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Dimensional inspection:</strong> CMM, gauges, optical systems, surface-finish checks, and first-article reporting.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Review our
                    <a href="/testing-inspection-capabilities" class="highlight-link">testing and inspection capabilities</a>
                    and
                    <a href="/quality-assurance-certifications" class="highlight-link">quality assurance and certifications</a>
                    for additional information.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="industry-applications">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Titanium Applications by Industry</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><a href="/aerospace" class="highlight-link">Aerospace:</a></strong> brackets, fittings, fasteners, shafts, engine-adjacent hardware, airframe structures, and propulsion components.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/space-defense" class="highlight-link">Space and defense:</a></strong> lightweight structures, housings, actuators, fasteners, UAV hardware, and corrosion-resistant components.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/medical" class="highlight-link">Medical and healthcare:</a></strong> instruments, housings, fixation hardware, and implantable components only where the exact grade, process, validation, and regulatory requirements are approved.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/oil-gas" class="highlight-link">Oil and gas:</a></strong> corrosion-resistant fasteners, valves, pump components, offshore hardware, and deepwater equipment.</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="/energy-power-generation" class="highlight-link">Energy and power generation:</a></strong> heat-exchanger hardware, turbine-related components, fasteners, and corrosion-resistant equipment parts.</li>
                    <li><strong>Chemical and marine processing:</strong> heat exchangers, piping components, vessels, pump parts, and seawater-service hardware.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

    </div>
</IndividualPageLayout>
